From 367f66f2f8b2c5ce6a982b9e67d9bf52109d66ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 21:20:57 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] sidebar: pin threads to a section of their own

The threads sidebar groups threads by project, so a thread someone keeps
returning to scrolls away as newer ones arrive, and a thread of a project
that is not open in this window cannot be reached from it at all. This
patch adds a Pinned section, the first consumer of 0048's store: story 032,
2026-10-09.

What it does. "Pin Thread" / "Unpin Thread" in a thread row's context menu,
a pin button on the hovered row, and agents_sidebar::TogglePinSelectedThread
(shift-p in the "ThreadsSidebar && not_searching" context of the three
default keymaps) toggle a pin. A pinned thread leaves its project group and
is listed after the last group under a collapsible "Pinned" header, the most
recently pinned first, each row labelled with its project's name; a project
whose shown threads are all pinned keeps its header. A draft (no message
sent yet) offers neither entry point. Pins are global, not per window: every
window shows the same section, threads of projects not open there included.
Activating such a row opens the project in this window when no window holds
it, and activates the thread the way History does when another window does.
An archived thread drops out of the section and keeps its pin; a pin naming
a thread Zeo does not know shows nothing. The filter, keyboard navigation,
the sticky header, rename and archive reach pinned rows as they reach group
rows.

How. rebuild_contents seeds seen_thread_ids with the pinned ids before the
group loop, so every expanded-group path (main path, linked worktrees, open
workspaces) skips them with no edit to those loops, while the group's
has_threads still counts them. After the loop, pinned_section.rs appends one
ListEntry::PinnedHeader and ordinary ListEntry::Thread rows built from the
store's global pinned() order and ThreadMetadataStore::entry -- not from the
window's project groups, which is what lets another project's thread
appear. Being ordinary Thread entries, selection, confirm, rename, archive
and the context menu work on them unchanged. The header is pushed onto
project_header_indices, so the sticky header and header navigation see it;
active_project_header_position and cycle_project skip it, since Pinned is
never the active project. EntryShape gains PinnedThread, because the project
label changes the row's height and an equal shape must mean an equal height.
The sidebar observes ThreadOrganizationStore the way it observes
ThreadMetadataStore, which is how a pin made in one window reaches the
others.

Failure: a pin or unpin the store refuses (a draft, a thread the metadata
store does not know) logs a warning naming the thread id and changes
nothing. pin/unpin read ThreadMetadataStore, so they are called only from UI
handlers, never inside its update. Without the store (try_global is None)
the sidebar draws no section and no entry point, exactly as before.

Upstream touch points: crates/sidebar/src/sidebar.rs (ListEntry and its
matches, EntryShape, ThreadEntry, rebuild_contents, render_sticky_header,
render_thread's hover slot and context menu, confirm and the collapse and
fold walks, cycle_project_impl, the actions! list and its .on_action,
Sidebar::new's observers); crates/sidebar/src/sidebar_tests.rs (helpers made
pub(super), a PinnedHeader arm in visible_entries_as_strings, the new
ThreadEntry field in its fixtures); one line in each of
assets/keymaps/default-{linux,macos,windows}.json. New:
crates/sidebar/src/pinned_section.rs, and sidebar_pinned_tests.rs (36
tests).

UNGROUPED and unconditional, right after 0048, whose store it consumes. The
claude-agent-acp-plus patches that also edit sidebar.rs (0028-0030) and the
keymaps (0035, 0043, 0044) apply after it and are generated on top of it.

Known limit: archiving a pinned row whose project is open only in another
window does not clear that window's agent panel.

Tests added 2026-10-10 (story 032 v4): a pinned row's Markdown entry across two windows.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
